# Agent Briefing — `{{agent-name}}` · {{phase}} · {{workflow-step}}

> **How to use:** this is the contract between the Orchestrator and a summoned agent
> (`core/orchestrator.md` §Invoking an agent). The Orchestrator fills in §Briefing (≤ 40 lines) and
> puts it **at the top** of the request — that's where a model follows the rules, not halfway
> through a 10 KB spec; the agent replies with **only** §Return (≤ 12 lines). The artifact lives in
> the `product/…` file — never in the response: **a return with the artifact pasted in is sent back
> to the agent** to redo in the right format; the Orchestrator reads the return, and the artifact is
> read by whoever consumes it downstream, or by the reviewer. The briefing **highlights** the spec,
> it doesn't replace it: the agent always has the full spec (read by the agent itself or compiled by
> the adapter — `adapters/claude-code.md` §Agents → subagents; `adapters/other-assistants.md`
> §Simulating subagents). In blind panels, N identical briefings go out and N returns come back; no
> agent sees another's (`core/orchestrator.md` §Parallelism). This template is never instantiated in
> `product/`: it lives in the request and the response; whatever from it should survive goes into the
> files each line of §Return points to. The return is a trace, not proof: the gate is still owned by
> whoever didn't produce the work (`core/quality-gates.md` §Anatomy of a gate), and the proof is in
> the artifact and the checklist.

